INT. MAYA'S BEDROOM - DAY

Maya lies in bed. She is nine years old. Her curly hair is messy from sleep.

She looks at the clock on her nightstand. It reads 4:00 PM.

Maya smiles. Today is the day.

She sits up. She grabs the edge of her lightweight manual wheelchair. With a smooth, practiced motion, she transfers from the bed into her seat.

She adjusts her teal windbreaker. She pulls on her fingerless gloves.

Hanging from her neck is her totem: a small adjustable wrench on a lanyard. She pats it for good luck.

MAYA
Time to get those gold casters.

Maya reaches under her seat and checks her toolkit. Everything is in place.

EXT. MAYA'S HOUSE - PORCH - DAY

Maya rolls out onto the front porch. She stops at the top of the ramp.

Her eyes go wide.

The street is gone. In its place is a rushing canal of muddy water. The flash flood has turned the neighborhood into a water park.

Across the street, Leo stands on his porch. He wears bright yellow rain boots.

LEO
Maya! The whole road is a river! We are totally stuck!

MAYA
I cannot be stuck! My package is at the post office! The mail truck leaves at five!

LEO
Forget the package! You cannot walk through that. Nobody can!

MAYA
I do not need to walk, Leo. I need to roll.

Maya looks at the rushing water. She looks at the curbs. They are now water ramps. She smiles. Her mind starts to race with angles and speed.

EXT. MAYA'S HOUSE - BACKYARD - DAY

Maya rolls quickly to the backyard shed. She finds an old foam surfboard leaning against the wall.

She grabs two plastic milk crates from near the trash cans.

She works fast. She places the surfboard on the grass. She straps the milk crates to the center of the board.

She rolls her wheelchair onto the board. Her main wheels fit perfectly between the crates. She uses her adjustable wrench to tighten the heavy-duty straps around her wheelchair frame.

Her wheelchair is now docked. It is a catamaran.

MAYA
Perfect fit. Let us see how you float.

EXT. FLOODED STREET - DAY

Maya lines up her rig at the edge of her driveway ramp.

Leo watches from his porch, his mouth wide open.

LEO
Maya, wait! That is crazy! What if you flip?

MAYA
As a wheelchair user with a spinal cord injury, I know exactly where my center of gravity is, Leo. Trust me. This board is not going anywhere.

Maya grips her wheel rims. She pushes forward. She rolls down the ramp and splashes into the water.

The surfboard dips, then rises. It floats!

Maya spins her main wheels. The rubber tread acts like paddlewheels. Water sprays behind her. She shoots forward down the flooded street.

LEO
Whoa! You are flying!

MAYA
See you at the finish line!

EXT. FLOODED BEND - DAY

Maya speeds down the street. The current is strong.

She approaches a sharp left turn. A flooded trash can floats in her path.

Maya leans her upper body to the right. She grips her right wheel rim to slow it down while spinning her left wheel fast.

The rig carves through the water. She dodges the trash can with inches to spare.

She laughs out loud. The wind blows through her curly hair.

EXT. MAIN STREET - LOG JAM - DAY

Maya turns onto Main Street. The water is deeper here.

Ahead, a giant fallen oak branch blocks the entire width of the street. It forms a wooden wall three feet high.

There is no way around it.

MAYA
Okay, Maya. Think. Three moves ahead.

She looks at the center of the log. It is wet and slick. It forms a natural ramp.

She grips her wheels tighter. She pumps her arms with all her strength. She gains speed.

MAYA
One, two, three, pop!

Maya shifts her weight backward. She pops a wheelie on her floating rig. The nose of the surfboard lifts out of the water.

The board hits the wet log. It slides up and over the smooth bark.

For a second, she is airborne.

She lands on the other side with a loud splash. The water sprays her teal windbreaker. She keeps her balance perfectly.

She looks back and pumps her fist.

MAYA
Yes!

EXT. POST OFFICE - DAY

The Riverdale Post Office sits on a high concrete hill. The steps are dry, but the parking lot is completely flooded.

A blue mail truck is idling in the driveway. The driver, Mr. Davis, is closing the back door.

MAYA
Mr. Davis! Wait!

Maya paddles her rig hard against the current. She reaches the bottom of the concrete steps.

She pulls her adjustable wrench from her lanyard. She quickly loosens the straps holding her wheelchair to the surfboard.

She locks her brakes. She transfers from the floating rig onto the dry concrete steps, moving with ease.

She pulls her manual wheelchair up onto the step next to her and transfers back in.

She rolls up the steps just as Mr. Davis turns around.

MR. DAVIS
Maya? How did you get here? The roads are closed!

MAYA
I took the scenic route. Do you have a package for Maya Patel?

Mr. Davis smiles and reaches into the front seat of his truck. He pulls out a small cardboard box.

MR. DAVIS
Just in time. I was about to drive this back to the main depot.

He hands her the box. Maya beams with joy.

INT. POST OFFICE PORCH - DAY

Maya sits on the dry porch. She rips open the cardboard box.

Inside are two limited-edition, gold-plated titanium caster wheels. They shine like jewelry.

MAYA
They are beautiful.

She takes her adjustable wrench from her lanyard. She unscrews her old gray front casters. She slides the new gold casters into place and tightens the bolts.

She spins the gold wheels. They spin silently and perfectly.

EXT. POST OFFICE - DAY

The floodwater is starting to recede. The sidewalk is wet but clear of deep water.

Maya rolls down the dry concrete ramp of the post office.

Her new golden casters catch the afternoon sun, casting bright yellow reflections on the wet ground.

She pushes forward. She rolls fast, smooth, and completely independent, heading back home.

THE END